('alict_01608', 12, 'General ICT', 'The answer is?', '["BW\u0305\u0305\u0305\u0305\u0305", "B\u0305 + M + W\u0305", "B\u0305 + (MW)", "MW", "W\u0305"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "B̅ + M + W̅".', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1608, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01609', 12, 'General ICT', 'A s control system shows its current fuel level as B3F16. The spaceship burns 2478 of fuel during a launch. How much fuel remains in the fuel tank after a launch?', '["AB316", "A5C16", "A4416", "A9816", "A1716"]'::jsonb, 4, '• Step Convert B3F16 to decimal. • Step Convert 2478 to decimal. • Step Subtract the two decimal values. 287910 - 16710 = 271210 • Step Convert 271210 to hexadecimal value', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1609, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01610', 12, 'General ICT', 'One of the principal inventors of the Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer (ENIAC)', '["Blaise Pascal", "Charles Babbage", "John V on Neumann", "Ada Augesta Lovelace", "John Presper Eckert"]'::jsonb, 5, 'The Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer (ENIAC) was one of the earliest general-purpose electronic digital computers. It was designed and developed in the United States during World War II to help the U.S. Army with artillery calculations', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1610, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01611', 12, 'General ICT', 'What is the answer of the calculation given below?', '["220", "8316", "2028", "8416", "1028"]'::jsonb, 3, 'let''s convert the hexadecimal value into binary. 6B16 6 B (11) 0 1 0 1 0 1 23 22 21 20 23 22 21 20 011010112 Since 101112 is already given in let''s do the calculation. 101112 0 1 0 1 0 1 + 1 0 1 0 1 0 let''s convert the binary value into octal', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1611, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01612', 12, 'General ICT', 'How many bits are in the mantissa of single precision of IEEE 754?', '["52", "1", "8", "23", "11"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The question asks about the number of bits in the mantissa of a single-precision floating-point number in the IEEE 754 standard. IEEE 754 single precision m%Yakh IEEE 754 Single-Precision Format (32-bit) • 1 bit for the sign • 8 bits for the exponent • 23 bits for the mantissa', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1612, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01613', 12, 'General ICT', 'How many bits are in the exponent of double precision of IEEE 754?', '["52", "1", "8", "23", "11"]'::jsonb, 5, 'The question asks about the number of bits in the exponent of a double-precision floating-point number in the IEEE 754 standard. IEEE 754 IEEE 754 Double-Precision Format (64-bit): • Sign 1 bit • 11 bits • 52 bits Explanation of each option 1: Incorrect', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1613, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_01614', 12, 'General ICT', 'Which is not a category of considering according to the size?', '["Super computers iqmsrs", "Analog computers", "Mainframe computers uyd", "Mini computers", "Micro computers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Option 1: Incorrect. This is a valid category based on size. These are the most powerful and largest computers. Option 2: Correct. This category is not based on size but on data processing type. Option 3: Incorrect. This is a valid category based on size', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'General ICT', NULL, 1614, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
