('alict_02173', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', '11101102 - ............. = 73 What is the correct answer that fits the blank here? fuys 2) Explanation', '["48", "D16", "55", "608", "1010012 X 0 1 0 1 46 0 1 0 1 0 Y 0 1 0 1 AND gate A B F 0 1 0 1 0 1 XOR gate A B F 0 1 0 1 0 Bitwise AND X 0 1 0 1 Y 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 Bitwise XOR X 0 1 0 1 Y 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 Then the appropriate value for the blank is decimal 45. miqj"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "D16". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2173, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02174', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Convert the hexadecimal number 2F.A₁₆ into and decimal. Which of the following is correct?', '["101111.101\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 47.625\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.46\u2088, 47.625\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.52\u2088, 47.65\u2081\u2080", "101111.110\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 47.75\u2081\u2080", "101111.101\u2082, 57.5\u2088, 46.625\u2081\u2080"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hexadecimal to Binary 2F.A 2 F A Binary 0 1 0 1 0 1 0 101111.10102 2.Binary to Octal Binary 1 0 1 0 1 0 Octal 5 7 5 0 57.5 8 3.Binary to Decimal Binary 1 0 1 0 1 0 Decimal 32 16 8 4 2 1 0.5 0.25 0.125 0.0625 32+8+4+2+1+0.5+0.125 = 47.625', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2174, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02175', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements about Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD) is true?', '["BCD is more efficient in terms of storage compared to pure binary representation", "Each decimal digit in BCD is represented by a 3-bit binary code. BCD", "The BCD representation of the decimal number 25 is 0010 0101. wxl 25 BCD 0010 0101", "BCD is primarily used for complex scientific calculations. BCD m%Odk", "All possible 4-bit binary combinations are valid in the BCD system. BCD"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In each decimal digit is converted to its 4-bit binary equivalent. 2 is represented as 0010, and 5 is represented as 0101. Combining them gives 0010 0101. BCD ixLHdxlhlau tys Option 1) BCD requires more bits to represent the same decimal number compared to pure binary', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2175, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02176', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements about a Full Adder is correct?', '["A Full Adder can add only two single-bit binary numbers", "A Full Adder produces two Sum and Carry. mQ\u00beK folla ksmojhs(", "A Full Adder requires only one Half Adder to be implemented", "A Full Adder does not use any logic gates", "A Full Adder cannot be cascaded to add multi-bit binary numbers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Full Adder always produces two Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cin Carry = (A·B) + (B·Cin) + (A·Cin) It calculates the sum of the three input bits and a carry-out to the next stage. th Option 1) A Full Adder adds three single-bit binary numbers (two input bits and a carry-in bit)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2176, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02177', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In a security alarm system implemented using an SR which of the following statements is correct?', '["The Set (S) input activates the and the Reset (R) input deactivates it. Set (S)", "The SR Latch can only store binary 0, not 1. SR Latch yg", "If both S and R are 1 the alarm enters a stable state. S R folu 1", "SR Latches cannot be used in security alarm systems", "The output of the SR Latch changes randomly regardless of inputs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'S = 1, R = 0 Activates (sets) the alarm. S = 0, R = 1 Deactivates (resets) the alarm. S = R = 0 Maintains the previous state. fmr S = R = 1 Considered invalid', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2177, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02178', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The 8-bit two''s complement number 11101010₂ is given. What is its equivalent decimal value in the original signed form?', '["-22", "-21", "-54", "-86", "+234"]'::jsonb, 1, '• Check sign bit (MSB) The number is 11101010₂. Since the MSB = 1, it''s a negative number in two''s complement form. MSB = 1 • Find the magnitude (original binary) To get the original number from two''s invert the bits and add 1', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2178, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02179', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given the binary number', '["18710, BB16", "18910, BC16", "18310, B716", "19110, BF16", "17510, AF16"]'::jsonb, 1, '101110112 in decimal 1 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 128 0 32 16 8 0 2 1 128 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 2 + 1 18710 101110112 in hexadecimal 1 0 1 0 1 23 22 21 20 23 22 21 20 8 0 2 1 8 0 2 1 8 + 2 + 1 = B 8 + 2 + 1 = B BB16', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2179, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02180', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Given two 8-bit unsigned binary numbers', '["000100102", "001011102", "010110102", "100100102", "110010002"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Step-by-Step explanation Convert A and B to decimal A = 001011012 0 1 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 0 32 0 8 4 0 1 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 4510 B = 000110112 0 1 0 1 27 26 25 24 23 22 21 20 0 16 8 0 2 1 16 + 8 + 2 + 1 2710 45 = 27 = 18 in binary 2 18 2 9 -0 2 4 -1 2 -0 1 -0 18 in 8-b', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2180, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02181', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Calculate the two''s complement of the number 110111012 represented by signed magnitude', '["152", "27", "254", "357", "239"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "152". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2181, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02182', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the purpose of ASCII code?', '["To compress images", "To represent characters using numbers", "To encrypt data", "To format hard drives", "To block viruses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The purpose of ASCII (American Standard Code for Information Interchange) is to represent such as and using numerical values. Each character is assigned a unique numeric code within a range of 0 to 127 in the standard ASCII set', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2182, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02183', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The decimal transition corresponding to BA5 hexadecimal transition and the 8-bit two''s complement representation corresponding to -17 are BA5 -17 g', '["2881, 101011112", "2891, 111011012", "2981, 111011112", "2981, 111011102", "2981, 111101112"]'::jsonb, 3, 'BA516 B A (10) 5 162 161 160 256 × 11 = 2816 16 × 10 = 160 1 × 5 = 5 2816 + 160 + 5 = 2981 298110 BA516 = 298110 let''s find the 8-bit two''s complement of -17', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2183, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02184', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Convert the binary number 1011.1012 into its and octal equivalents. Which of the following options correctly represents all three conversions?', '["1011.1012 = 11.75 = B.C16 = 13.68", "1011.1012 = 11.625 = B.A16 = 13.58", "1011.1012 = 10.625 = A.A16 = 12.58", "1011.1012 = 11.375 = B.616 = 13.38", "1011.1012 = 11.625 = B.916 = 13.78"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Step 1: Binary to Decimal Conversion 1( Binary 1 0 1 0 1 Decimal 8 4 2 1 0.5 0.25 0.125 8+2+1+0.5+0.125 = 11.625 Step 2 :Binary to Hexadecimal Conversion 2( Binary 1011.1012 1011 1010 Hexa 8 4 2 1 8 4 2 1 8+2+1 8+2 = A B', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2184, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02185', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In an 8-bit 2''s complement what is the range of decimal values that can be represented?', '["-128 to +128", "-127 to +127", "-128 to +127", "-127 to +128", "-256 to +255"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "-128 to +127". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2185, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02186', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the decimal value of the answer obtained after performing the bitwise XOR operation on 11011011 and 10111010?', '["1100001", "96", "405", "97", "251"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "97". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2186, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02187', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'If A = what would be the relevant ASCII code of A represented in octal?', '["1168 1578 1548 1418 1568", "1178 1548 1578 1568 1418", "1418 1578 1548 1178 1568", "1168 1578 1548 1418 1578", "1168 1558 1548 1578 1568"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Here in the question it has given that N - 78, o - 111 - 108, a - 97 and n - 110. fuys m%Yakfha N - 78, o - 111 - 108, a - 97 n - 110 78 111 108 97 110 82 81 80 82 81 80 82 81 80 82 81 80 82 81 80 64 8 1 64 8 1 64 8 1 64 8 1 64 8 1 6 1 5 7 1 5 4 1 4 1 5 6 1168 1578 1548 1418 1108', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2187, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02188', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the most simplified form of A. (B + C) + A. (B + C) + A. B. C A. (B + C) + A. (B + C) + A. B. C', '["A. B. C", "A + B + C", "B + C", "A. (B + C)", "None of the lsisjla determine the correct ASCII value for \"CEDAB\", we need to convert each letter to its ASCII binary equivalent. \"CEDAB\" ASCII w.h tys ASCII iudk njg values for the letters"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A. B. C". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2188, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02189', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'A programmer wants to send the word "HELLO" from one computer to another. The computers only understand so each letter is converted into a unique binary code before transmission. This system the programmer is using is', '["Unicode", "ASCII", "Binary Compression", "Hexadecimal", "Machine Learning"]'::jsonb, 5, 'To find the two''s complement of a binary we need to perform the following Convert the number to its one''s complement by flipping all the bits (0s become 1s and 1s become 0s). Add 1 to the one''s complement to obtain the two''s complement', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2189, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02190', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Answer 2''s complement of the value obtained when the following statement is trivialized?', '["001011112", "110101112", "010101012", "110101002", "001010112 Statement"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Grid computing is a set of computers working together to perform a specific task. lsysmhla The workload is divided among the computers and each computer has its own contribution toward the common goal. Its advantages are as follows. tys jdis • Process is quick', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2190, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02191', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following statements about binary conversion is correct?', '["Binary conversion changes a number into a base-16 system using only digits 0-9 and letters A-F", "Binary conversion represents numbers using only two typically 0 and 1, which computers can process", "Binary conversion is used to encrypt data for secure communication", "Binary conversion converts text directly into images for storage", "Binary conversion increases the decimal value of a number by multiplying it by 2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "Binary conversion represents numbers using only two typically 0 and 1, which computers can process". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2191, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02192', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What can be considered as data validation method', '["A only. A", "A and B only. A B", "A and C only. A C", "A and D only. A D", "All of the What is the two''s complement representation of -79?"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "A and C only. A C". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2192, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02193', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What octal number is equivalent to the decimal 0.3125?', '["1.248", "0.258", "0.248", "0.1258", "0.58"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct answer: "0.248". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2193, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02194', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is equals to -7610 + AC16 ?', '["1258", "101101112", "1408", "5916", "10610 Converting hexadecimal to 160 16 1 160+12 17210 Let''s convert this answer into other bases. fuu lr the correct answer is 1408. the correct answer number is 1408 3 Questions 4 and 5 are based on the below circuit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "1258". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2194, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02195', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What will be the Q output if SET is turned to 0 ?', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "C only. C", "A and B only. A B", "A and C only. A C Converting decimal to binary"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct answer: "A only. A". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2195, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02196', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'What is the result of the following arithmetic operation performed in hexadecimal notation?', '["B5D", "2493", "FEBD", "A2F", "None of the lsisjla Explanation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Adding AB2F16 and 3D7E16 AB2F16 3D7E16 A B 2 F 3 D 7 E 8 A D Subtracting C41A16 from E8AD16 E 8 A D C 4 1 A 2 4 9 3', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2196, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02197', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'The number 111011102 is expressed in signed magnitude representation. Two''s complement of that number is equal hk olajd tu foflys wkqmQrlhg iudk jkafka" A. 100100102 B. 2228 C. 9216 Answer : 4) Let''s find the two''s complement of the binary number wxl 111011102 foflys fidhuq'' • Change all 0s to 1s and all 1s to 0s', '["A only. A", "B only. B", "A and B only. A B", "All of the above", "None of the lsisjla 2228 is also equal to the 2228 o 0 1 0 1 0 23 22 21 20 23 22 21 20 1 x 8 0 1 x 1 0 2 0 9 9216is also equal to the 9216ixLHdjg o since B and C are the correct answer is answer number B C wxl 4 Questions 4 and 5 are based on the below circuit. m%Yak wxl 4 5 that an alarm is fixed to the Q output that when Q outputs a true value the alarm will sound and turns off when Q = 0. Q lr nj lrkak. Q kdo Q = 0 th What will be the Q output if SET is turned to 0 ?"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "All of the above". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2197, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02198', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following answer represents bitwise XNOR operation of the two binary numbers 110010112 and 111010102?', '["110111102", "100111102", "111000102", "101110112", "1101111"]'::jsonb, 1, 'XZ̅̅̅̅(X̅ + Z)(Y + Y̅) (X̅ + Z̅)(X̅ + Z)(Y + Y̅) De Morgan''s theory (X̅ + Z̅)(X̅ + Z)', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2198, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02199', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'In 1944, IBM along with Howard Aiken of Harvard University created the Automatic Sequence Controlled Calculator. This made it possible to solve complex math problems without an operator. What was the name of this machine? 1944 whslka iu. IBM iud.u tlaj iajhxl%Sh wkql%uk fuu.ska lrjkafkla . ku 2) Explanation', '["ABC The Atanasoff-Berry developed in the late 1930s and early was an earlier digital computer but not related to the ASCC or Mark I. 1930 w.Nd.fha 1940", "EDV AC The Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer was an early binary-stored program but it was developed in the late 1940s", "IBM 704 This was one of IBM''s early introduced in 1954, but it came much later than the Mark I. 1954", "EDSAC The Electronic Delay Storage Automatic Calculator was one of the first stored -program in the late 1940s at the University of m%udo iajhxl%Sh hkq 1940 ,o ,o Mark I is the correct ud\u00bela I", "ABC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct answer: "EDV AC The Electronic Discrete Variable Automatic Computer was an early binary-stored program but it was developed in the late 1940s". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2199, 'A/L Past Paper Series'),
('alict_02200', 2, 'Number Systems & Data Representation', 'Which of the following is not a valid Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) value?', '["Magnetic tapes", "Floppy disk", "SD cards", "Punch cards", "None of the above"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Correct answer: "Punch cards". This follows from converting/calculating the given number(s) using standard place-value (base) arithmetic.', NULL, NULL, 'medium', 'Number Systems & Data Representation', NULL, 2200, 'A/L Past Paper Series')
